WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.186 --> 00:00:04.767
Hello everyone! Welcome back to the "Mathematical Experiments" channel.

00:00:04.767 --> 00:00:11.413
Last time, we studied the Pi function – The number of prime numbers ≤ x.

00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:15.845
And we got a quite complex formula for approximating this Pi function,

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:19.245
which imperfectly approximates the Pi function.

00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:21.751
When we subtract this approximation from Pi,

00:00:21.751 --> 00:00:24.440
we get this noise in the result,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.257
which oscillates from -0.5 to +0.5 approximately,

00:00:29.257 --> 00:00:33.071
but sometimes goes beyond this range.

00:00:33.071 --> 00:00:38.466
Today, we will continue studying this noise and try to decompose it into components,

00:00:38.466 --> 00:00:41.530
but this time, we will deal not with the Pi function,

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:48.698
but with various sums over prime numbers and powers of prime numbers.

00:00:48.698 --> 00:00:50.581
Why is this necessary?

00:00:50.581 --> 00:01:00.000
Where does this idea of taking sums over prime numbers and powers of prime numbers come from?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.591
I tried to find a simple explanation for where this desire might come from,

00:01:06.591 --> 00:01:11.195
and I found nothing simpler than an explanation through the zeta function.

00:01:11.195 --> 00:01:14.340
We naturally came to the zeta function with you

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.194
when we studied partial sums of natural number powers.

00:01:18.194 --> 00:01:26.396
And when we came to the Euler-Maclaurin formula, an approximate calculation of partial sums,

00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:29.460
there we also encountered the zeta function,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.520
which is defined in this way for s &gt; 1,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:40.913
and for other s, it is defined as the analytic continuation of this function.

00:01:40.913 --> 00:01:48.327
So... the zeta function of s can be written as a product of these brackets.

00:01:48.327 --> 00:01:52.974
The product is taken over prime numbers.

00:01:52.974 --> 00:01:54.351
Look how interesting it is.

00:01:54.351 --> 00:01:57.428
Initially, the sum is taken over natural numbers,

00:01:57.428 --> 00:02:02.619
but this sum can be represented as a product over prime numbers p.

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:13.969
Each of these brackets (next step) can be written as 1/(1 - 1/(p^s)).

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:20.628
What kind of formula is this? It is the formula of an infinite geometric progression.

00:02:20.628 --> 00:02:22.070
Thus,

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:29.623
the zeta function can be represented as 1 divided by the product.

00:02:29.623 --> 00:02:32.221
And here is the next clever trick.

00:02:32.221 --> 00:02:39.653
Let's take the logarithm of the zeta function and then take the derivative of that.

00:02:39.653 --> 00:02:43.595
What's the idea behind taking the logarithm and then the derivative?

00:02:43.595 --> 00:02:50.033
The thing is, it's a very fruitful idea that allows you to crack any function f.

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.293
Why?

00:02:51.293 --> 00:02:57.904
Because cracking a function f means understanding its zeros and poles.

00:02:57.904 --> 00:03:01.323
I also have a video on the channel about that.

00:03:01.323 --> 00:03:02.381
So, what happens?

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:09.549
If the function f has a zero or pole at point s0,

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:12.431
then when we take the logarithm of f,

00:03:12.431 --> 00:03:17.440
this pole or zero separates from the rest.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:24.634
And when we take the derivative, this pole or zero becomes a pole.

00:03:27.279 --> 00:03:31.930
So... when you take the logarithmic derivative of the function f,

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:38.054
all its zeros and poles turn into ordinary poles of degree 1,

00:03:38.054 --> 00:03:45.496
whose numerator is equal to the degree of that pole or zero of the original function f.

00:03:45.496 --> 00:03:52.502
Therefore, this idea of taking the logarithmic derivative is very fruitful for cracking functions.

00:03:52.502 --> 00:03:57.045
And what will happen if we take the logarithmic derivative of this thing?

00:03:57.045 --> 00:04:01.141
We take the logarithm of this ratio...

00:04:01.141 --> 00:04:04.683
Well... it's the same as taking the logarithm of this product with a minus sign.

00:04:04.683 --> 00:04:07.779
The logarithm of a product becomes the sum of logarithms.

00:04:07.779 --> 00:04:09.343
And we take the derivative with respect to s.

00:04:09.343 --> 00:04:11.363
What do we get?

00:04:11.363 --> 00:04:13.655
Here I wrote this down.

00:04:13.655 --> 00:04:20.391
We get that each bracket gives

00:04:20.391 --> 00:04:22.910
this term.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:28.930
The derivative of the logarithm of some function is equal to 1 divided by that function, multiplied by the derivative.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:30.524
Here I wrote it.

00:04:30.524 --> 00:04:35.523
Implicitly, there is a 1 here, divided by the zeta function.

00:04:35.523 --> 00:04:40.317
And here I drew the derivative of this function.

00:04:40.317 --> 00:04:47.607
Thus, the logarithmic derivative of the zeta function equals...

00:04:47.607 --> 00:04:52.745
Well... without the minus sign, because here I have 1 divided by the zeta function.

00:04:52.745 --> 00:05:00.998
So, we get a sum over powers of prime numbers

00:05:00.998 --> 00:05:08.838
and each power of a prime number is weighted by the logarithm of "p".

00:05:08.838 --> 00:05:16.342
Notice that here, inside the logarithm, there's no "s" and no "k".

00:05:16.342 --> 00:05:22.176
And essentially, I'm going to work with this sum now.

00:05:22.176 --> 00:05:29.386
Let me demonstrate on a simple small example the sums that I'm going to calculate today.

00:05:29.386 --> 00:05:31.467
So, here, we get 2 lists:

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:36.132
the first list consists of prime numbers and their powers.

00:05:36.132 --> 00:05:41.855
That is, not only 2, but also 4, 8, 16, etc.

00:05:41.855 --> 00:05:46.459
Not only 3, but also 9, 27, etc.

00:05:46.459 --> 00:05:49.938
The second list contains the prime numbers themselves.

00:05:49.938 --> 00:05:52.155
And what do I want to calculate today?

00:05:52.155 --> 00:05:56.882
I want to calculate, for example, this sum.

00:05:56.882 --> 00:06:03.869
Prime number or power of a prime number multiplied by the logarithm of that prime number.

00:06:03.869 --> 00:06:09.324
Let me calculate these two arrays up to 1000

00:06:09.324 --> 00:06:12.481
and add up, for example, this.

00:06:12.481 --> 00:06:16.052
This is the sum of the logarithm of "p" multiplyed by "pp".

00:06:16.052 --> 00:06:21.420
"pp" - I denote the power of the prime number, and "p" - the prime number itself.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.322
This is how this sum looks.

00:06:24.322 --> 00:06:27.329
Looks like a quadratic dependence.

00:06:27.329 --> 00:06:31.204
Let's divide it by "pp".

00:06:31.204 --> 00:06:35.333
Indeed, it's quadratic, because after dividing by "pp," we're left with something linear.

00:06:35.333 --> 00:06:38.580
And I even tried to approximate it here.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:45.665
But let me divide it by the square instead.

00:06:45.665 --> 00:06:47.047
What do I see here?

00:06:47.047 --> 00:06:49.584
It really converges to some constant.

00:06:49.584 --> 00:06:54.142
This constant looks very much like 0.5.

00:06:54.142 --> 00:06:55.857
Next...

00:06:55.857 --> 00:07:01.860
Well... I'm an experienced guy. Let me multiply it all by the square root of "pp".

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.647
"pp" is sort of our x in this video.

00:07:06.647 --> 00:07:08.250
Here's what we get.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:11.699
And I see a changing noise.

00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:16.706
More or less, it lies in the range (-0.5, 0.5)

00:07:16.706 --> 00:07:18.452
Okay.

00:07:18.452 --> 00:07:19.621
Another example:

00:07:19.621 --> 00:07:24.532
we can take the sum of Log[p] divided by "pp".

00:07:24.532 --> 00:07:28.223
And here we can see that the dependence is more logarithmic.

00:07:28.223 --> 00:07:31.429
When we subtract the logarithm, here's what we see.

00:07:31.429 --> 00:07:36.155
A noise that converges to some number, maybe -0.57.

00:07:36.155 --> 00:07:39.954
Add this 0.57

00:07:39.954 --> 00:07:49.554
and again, we see noise that lies more or less in the range (-0.5, 0.5), etc.

00:07:49.554 --> 00:07:58.475
In short, I've already precalculated the powers of prime numbers less than or equal to 50 million.

00:07:58.475 --> 00:08:03.142
I've also calculated the prime numbers themselves, 3 million of them.

00:08:03.142 --> 00:08:05.343
And I've calculated various sums.

00:08:05.343 --> 00:08:07.944
Today we will be interested in these sums.

00:08:07.944 --> 00:08:12.769
But in the process, I also calculated sums over prime numbers.

00:08:12.769 --> 00:08:18.828
Here we have two arrays of the same size, "pp" and "p".

00:08:18.828 --> 00:08:24.709
We consider our sum function as a function of "pp".

00:08:24.709 --> 00:08:27.367
Based on what is written in the "pp" array.

00:08:27.367 --> 00:08:31.738
But during summation, we can use both the power of the prime number "pp",

00:08:31.738 --> 00:08:35.217
and the prime number "p" itself, the power of which we take.

00:08:35.217 --> 00:08:37.356
Today, I'm interested in sums with logarithms.

00:08:37.356 --> 00:08:42.731
Simply the sum of logarithms of "p" over all powers of prime numbers.

00:08:42.731 --> 00:08:51.383
Summ "pp" multiplyed by logarithm "p" is denoted as "sumPPp1Log".

00:08:51.383 --> 00:09:03.531
I use the letter "r" to denote negative powers, when I take 1/p or 1/(p^2).

00:09:03.531 --> 00:09:09.490
Now, let's try to crack these sums.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:13.837
Let's move to this place.

00:09:13.837 --> 00:09:16.657
First.

00:09:16.657 --> 00:09:19.467
We take the sum...

00:09:19.467 --> 00:09:21.750
I remind you of this notation.

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:30.975
This is sort of the sum of "1/p", multiplied by the logarithm of "p".

00:09:30.975 --> 00:09:32.392
Or you can write it like this:

00:09:32.392 --> 00:09:38.166
this sum is in this list.

00:09:38.166 --> 00:09:41.554
The function ListQPart takes a part of this list.

00:09:41.554 --> 00:09:43.392
The list itself is quite large.

00:09:43.392 --> 00:09:48.574
How many elements do I have there... I don't even remember.

00:09:48.574 --> 00:09:51.812
3 million elements.

00:09:51.812 --> 00:09:59.243
And I don't want to plot a graph of 3 million points because Mathematica will lag.

00:09:59.243 --> 00:10:05.390
I'm taking only 10 thousand points from this quantity.

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:09.325
I take points that are approximately in a geometric progression.

00:10:09.325 --> 00:10:16.809
Their indices form an approximate geometric progression from 1 to 3000000.

00:10:16.809 --> 00:10:21.178
Lets draw it...

00:10:21.178 --> 00:10:24.850
Here I have the answer right away,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:27.033
but it wasn't difficult to get it.

00:10:27.033 --> 00:10:30.582
How is it done?

00:10:30.582 --> 00:10:37.875
First, I simply plot the function y[x],

00:10:37.875 --> 00:10:41.385
or rather, y of the logarithm of x.

00:10:41.385 --> 00:10:43.720
#[1] is our x,

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.542
#[2] is our y,

00:10:45.542 --> 00:10:50.109
that is, the sum up to x.

00:10:50.109 --> 00:10:54.907
Here we can see that y is approximately equal to x,

00:10:54.907 --> 00:10:56.907
but, in fact, it's not x, but the logarithm of x.

00:10:56.907 --> 00:11:04.479
So, it's very natural to subtract this logarithm.

00:11:04.479 --> 00:11:06.445
We immediately get noise.

00:11:06.445 --> 00:11:14.188
Remember how long we struggled before extracting the noise from Pi?

00:11:14.188 --> 00:11:18.823
Here, all of this happened in one step from the first try.

00:11:18.823 --> 00:11:21.410
That's why I chose this sum over powers of prime numbers.

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:24.000
The results are much easier to get here.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.331
Similar results are gotten when summing over prime numbers,

00:11:28.331 --> 00:11:32.002
but there you need to perform two actions,

00:11:32.002 --> 00:11:34.764
while here it's just one.

00:11:34.764 --> 00:11:41.258
Let's multiply this noise by √x.

00:11:41.258 --> 00:11:42.731
So...

00:11:42.731 --> 00:11:44.965
But before multiplying it,

00:11:44.965 --> 00:11:47.649
first we need to see that it's shifted,

00:11:47.649 --> 00:11:51.168
and I remove this shift.

00:11:51.168 --> 00:11:56.981
I add 0.577

00:11:56.981 --> 00:12:04.998
Here wo go... shifted it to the center, to one, but here I also need to...

00:12:04.998 --> 00:12:09.675
To do like this...

00:12:10.222 --> 00:12:16.285
The thing is, an experienced mathematician recognizes this constant as EulerGamma,

00:12:16.285 --> 00:12:24.426
and I recognized it immediately and wrote it down as well.

00:12:24.426 --> 00:12:29.350
And as a result, I got this noise.

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:35.795
Sum of Log[p] divided by pp.

00:12:35.795 --> 00:12:39.578
This is just the logarithm of pp

00:12:39.578 --> 00:12:42.672
minus EulerGamma.

00:12:42.672 --> 00:12:46.455
Okay... moving on.

00:12:46.455 --> 00:12:51.029
Now, the sum of just logarithms of p.

00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:57.488
That is, pp is present with a power of 0, so it's written as 0 here.

00:12:57.488 --> 00:13:00.174
What do we do here?

00:13:00.174 --> 00:13:02.966
The same procedure.

00:13:02.966 --> 00:13:08.786
First, we draw the plot of y of the logarithm of x,

00:13:08.786 --> 00:13:12.651
see a growing graph,

00:13:12.651 --> 00:13:16.358
Though...

00:13:16.358 --> 00:13:19.625
it's not enough to divide by the logarithm of x.

00:13:19.625 --> 00:13:21.559
We divide by x itself.

00:13:21.559 --> 00:13:26.928
We see that it tends to one.

00:13:26.928 --> 00:13:32.679
All these constants are very easy to guess, they turn out to be round numbers here.

00:13:32.679 --> 00:13:35.672
Then we multiply by √x,

00:13:35.672 --> 00:13:38.529
and I did that right here.

00:13:38.529 --> 00:13:44.412
And we get a nice, pretty noise,

00:13:44.412 --> 00:13:45.408
familiar to us already.

00:13:45.408 --> 00:13:47.793
They all look very similar.

00:13:47.793 --> 00:13:49.272
Here we had the same noise as well.

00:13:49.272 --> 00:13:54.211
See this cluster at the very end at the bottom?

00:13:54.211 --> 00:13:57.257
There's a similar cluster here.

00:13:57.257 --> 00:13:59.105
Let's move on...

00:13:59.105 --> 00:14:03.429
This sum is pp multiplied by the logarithm of p.

00:14:03.429 --> 00:14:06.248
Here we divide by x^2

00:14:06.248 --> 00:14:11.319
and find that we need to subtract 0.5,

00:14:11.319 --> 00:14:15.954
to get an unbiased noise.

00:14:15.954 --> 00:14:19.771
Next, we see that we need to subtract 0.3333,

00:14:19.771 --> 00:14:23.109
assume it's 1/3.

00:14:23.109 --> 00:14:25.409
Moving on.

00:14:27.992 --> 00:14:33.575
Here the answers are easy to guess.

00:14:33.575 --> 00:14:35.423
This last thing.

00:14:35.423 --> 00:14:42.000
We sum (pp^4) multiplied by the logarithm of p.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:48.000
We need to divide by x^5 and subtract 1/5.

00:14:51.314 --> 00:14:53.762
What we've ultimately got.

00:14:53.762 --> 00:15:00.000
We got five noises,

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:06.182
six, actually, if we consider that first noise where I took 1/pp,

00:15:06.182 --> 00:15:09.844
and they all look very similar to each other.

00:15:09.844 --> 00:15:11.304
Wonderful!

00:15:11.304 --> 00:15:13.537
Now let's...

00:15:13.537 --> 00:15:15.568
What is naturally to do here...

00:15:15.568 --> 00:15:18.931
We need to subtract one noise from another.

00:15:18.931 --> 00:15:25.025
I'll subtract the noise for power 1 from the noise for power 0.

00:15:25.025 --> 00:15:29.328
The noise for power 2 from the noise for power 1, and so on.

00:15:29.328 --> 00:15:36.152
So, I'll take neighboring noises and subtract one from the other.

00:15:36.152 --> 00:15:39.134
What is CatXYs?

00:15:39.134 --> 00:15:43.315
It is assumed that we combine two lists of XY pairs,

00:15:43.315 --> 00:15:51.334
and their x-values are the same, so we can take any of the x's (x1, x2 and x3).

00:15:56.959 --> 00:15:58.793
Here...

00:15:58.793 --> 00:16:01.973
Lets draw it...

00:16:01.973 --> 00:16:04.581
What a wonderful picture.

00:16:04.581 --> 00:16:08.870
This is the key picture in this video.

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:12.228
The first noise (blue, original),

00:16:12.228 --> 00:16:19.518
it goes beyond the bounds of our rectangle on the xy plane.

00:16:19.518 --> 00:16:29.318
See? Here, the Y-axis is taken in the range (-0.04, 0.03).

00:16:29.318 --> 00:16:37.948
The first noise, it has a large amplitude of about 0.5.

00:16:37.948 --> 00:16:40.592
These 4 noises

00:16:40.592 --> 00:16:44.418
make up this plot, for example.

00:16:44.418 --> 00:16:47.244
Also, the purple plot.

00:16:47.244 --> 00:16:48.796
Also, the red and blue plots.

00:16:48.796 --> 00:16:54.262
The red and blue plots, they are hidden behind the purple one.

00:16:55.904 --> 00:16:59.721
Further, there are three more things drawn here.

00:16:59.721 --> 00:17:02.822
What are these things?

00:17:02.822 --> 00:17:08.023
Here, I took, essentially,

00:17:08.023 --> 00:17:10.085
the second noise

00:17:10.085 --> 00:17:11.406
and the first one.

00:17:11.406 --> 00:17:14.119
And I subtracted this noise from that noise.

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:15.399
From this noise,

00:17:15.399 --> 00:17:16.294
this noise.

00:17:16.294 --> 00:17:18.770
Something like that.

00:17:20.958 --> 00:17:23.739
Look at how it happens.

00:17:23.739 --> 00:17:27.198
This can be explained as follows.

00:17:27.198 --> 00:17:30.890
I numbered these three noises (there are three of them)

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:35.737
as noise #[2],

00:17:35.737 --> 00:17:38.348
#[3].

00:17:38.348 --> 00:17:41.078
Let's also call this #[3].

00:17:41.078 --> 00:17:43.524
And let this be #[4].

00:17:43.524 --> 00:17:45.848
And now when I subtract from this difference

00:17:45.848 --> 00:17:47.787
this difference,

00:17:47.787 --> 00:17:53.082
I get exactly this expression.

00:17:53.082 --> 00:17:55.252
That's the explanation.

00:17:55.252 --> 00:18:00.651
So now I have three levels of noise.

00:18:00.651 --> 00:18:04.830
The original noises we had before.

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:10.265
Noises of the form "difference of original noises".

00:18:10.265 --> 00:18:15.789
And noises of the form "difference of these previous-level noises".

00:18:15.789 --> 00:18:18.734
And of course, we can keep going further.

00:18:18.734 --> 00:18:25.557
This little yellow sine wave here is precisely one of these three plots.

00:18:25.557 --> 00:18:27.890
The second one is red,

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:33.649
and the third one is blue. It is hidden behind the yellow one. It's not visible here.

00:18:33.649 --> 00:18:38.191
At the very beginning, it slightly shows, and then disappears.

00:18:40.532 --> 00:18:51.129
Here, I tried to draw the yellow noise in a large scale.

00:18:51.129 --> 00:18:54.000
Here it just turned blue.

00:18:54.000 --> 00:18:56.446
Let's move on.

00:18:56.446 --> 00:19:02.236
Now we can take the noises of this third level and subtract one from the other.

00:19:02.236 --> 00:19:04.822
Subtracting.

00:19:04.822 --> 00:19:09.213
Here, we essentially concatenate four y's,

00:19:09.213 --> 00:19:16.614
and refer to these y's as #[2], #[3], #[4] and #[5].

00:19:16.614 --> 00:19:21.887
Here we get a formula, which is nothing but (a - b)^3.

00:19:21.887 --> 00:19:24.819
These are binomial coefficients.

00:19:24.819 --> 00:19:27.417
And finally...

00:19:27.417 --> 00:19:30.532
So... some unnecessary actions were performed here.

00:19:30.532 --> 00:19:36.000
Let me erase them.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:37.947
Look...

00:19:37.947 --> 00:19:41.001
We take the noise of the fourth level

00:19:41.001 --> 00:19:42.930
and it no longer looks like noise.

00:19:42.930 --> 00:19:46.957
It's some smooth curve that tends to zero.

00:19:46.957 --> 00:19:52.151
Let's try to guess the relationship for this smooth curve.

00:19:52.151 --> 00:19:54.152
What are we doing here...

00:19:54.152 --> 00:19:58.300
We repeat the entire expression, only here we write

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:02.647
some function of x.

00:20:02.647 --> 00:20:06.122
So... forgot the minus sign.

00:20:06.122 --> 00:20:08.203
Here we go...

00:20:08.203 --> 00:20:10.314
Here you could try

00:20:10.314 --> 00:20:12.516
just #[1],

00:20:12.516 --> 00:20:14.243
exponential of #[1],

00:20:14.243 --> 00:20:16.962
or exponential of 0.5#[1].

00:20:16.962 --> 00:20:20.650
And it turns out that this option is successful.

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:23.825
It corresponds to the yellow plot.

00:20:23.825 --> 00:20:28.156
Only you can see that it needs to be multiplied by something.

00:20:28.156 --> 00:20:29.446
Multiplying a bit more...

00:20:29.446 --> 00:20:37.108
and a bit more... -1.83 or -1.84

00:20:37.108 --> 00:20:40.344
Here we go ... somewhere -1.83, -1.84.

00:20:40.344 --> 00:20:43.074
Alright...

00:20:43.074 --> 00:20:49.837
Subtracting this expression with a minus sign. We get a plus.

00:20:51.843 --> 00:20:53.258
So...

00:20:53.258 --> 00:20:55.673
Now we don't need this yellow approximation anymore.

00:20:55.673 --> 00:20:58.646
I'm removing the 0.5 here.

00:20:58.646 --> 00:21:01.517
Removing the plus sign.

00:21:01.517 --> 00:21:04.662
I see that it goes too far.

00:21:04.662 --> 00:21:09.540
0.5 – still goes too far.

00:21:13.158 --> 00:21:16.397
Perhaps this is the best option.

00:21:16.397 --> 00:21:23.129
So, I took the noise of the 4th level and started seeing analytical components in it.

00:21:23.129 --> 00:21:26.700
And I extract and subtract these components.

00:21:26.700 --> 00:21:30.362
This is our standard procedure.

00:21:30.362 --> 00:21:33.811
Here, I just drew zero.

00:21:33.811 --> 00:21:38.299
Well, let's try 1 divided by 1.5.

00:21:38.299 --> 00:21:41.049
Well, probably it is right.

00:21:43.572 --> 00:21:46.742
Alright... and then we subtract.

00:21:46.742 --> 00:21:50.678
Subtracting...

00:21:50.678 --> 00:21:54.531
And here I write zero.

00:21:54.531 --> 00:22:00.000
Alright... enough of subtracting these analytical components.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.689
We can already see a sine wave here.

00:22:03.689 --> 00:22:05.690
Writing...

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:15.614
Cosine of #1.

00:22:15.614 --> 00:22:17.857
The amplitude here is very small.

00:22:17.857 --> 00:22:19.664
How much...

00:22:19.664 --> 00:22:21.874
So much?

00:22:23.819 --> 00:22:27.250
More frequency...

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:30.000
This is too much...

00:22:34.013 --> 00:22:37.919
Well, probably, with a minus sign.

00:22:37.919 --> 00:22:39.934
Yes.

00:22:39.934 --> 00:22:41.407
But I didn't guess the amplitude.

00:22:41.407 --> 00:22:43.457
Something like that.

00:22:43.457 --> 00:22:45.994
Subtracting this sine wave.

00:22:50.007 --> 00:22:55.929
Here I already have the answer drawn.

00:22:58.179 --> 00:23:04.390
I adjusted the frequency of the sine wave here

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:06.585
and also the offset.

00:23:13.394 --> 00:23:20.550
So that this remaining blue noise is as small as possible.

00:23:23.499 --> 00:23:32.419
Thus, we have reached the number 14.1.

00:23:32.419 --> 00:23:38.634
This is the first nontrivial zero of the zeta function.

00:23:38.634 --> 00:23:45.257
In fact, in this video, I wanted to reach the zeros of the zeta function by the shortest path.

00:23:45.257 --> 00:23:51.138
Using elementary methods, simply analyzing these sums over powers of prime numbers,

00:23:51.138 --> 00:23:57.638
we managed to reach a sine wave

00:23:57.638 --> 00:24:14.646
with a frequency equal to the value of the imaginary component of the first zero of the zeta function on the vertical line 0.5

00:24:14.646 --> 00:24:22.260
I have a video where I tried to reach the values of the zeta function at points 2, 4, etc.,

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:26.297
as well as at fractional values like 0.5 or -0.5.

00:24:26.297 --> 00:24:31.540
And here, for the first time, I reached the zeros of the zeta function.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:34.628
Such an interesting observation.

00:24:34.628 --> 00:24:39.141
Such a hierarchy of noises.

00:24:39.141 --> 00:24:47.836
And at the fourth step of removing the fourth component, we saw a sine wave

00:24:47.836 --> 00:24:54.963
that corresponds to the first nontrivial zero of the zeta function.

00:24:54.963 --> 00:24:57.862
The rest is up to you.

00:24:57.862 --> 00:25:05.476
You probably guess that this remainder can be further expanded into a sum of sine waves.

00:25:05.476 --> 00:25:07.800
Try doing it yourself.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:10.651
Leave comments, otherwise, it's boring for me.

00:25:10.651 --> 00:25:12.878
Good luck to everyone! Bye!

